Slope of the line,
$\begin{aligned}
& y-3 x+18=0 \\
& \Rightarrow y=3 x-18 \Rightarrow \text{ Slope }\left(m_1\right)=3
\end{aligned}$
and angle $(\theta)=60^{{}^{\circ}}\begin{aligned}
& \text{ so, } \tan 60^{{}^{\circ}}=\left|\frac{m_1-m_2}{1+m_1 m_2}\right| \\
& \Rightarrow \quad \sqrt{3}=\left|\frac{3-m_2}{1+3 m_2}\right|
\end{aligned}\Rightarrow \frac{3-m_2}{1+3 m_2}= \pm \sqrt{3}$
Either, $\frac{3-m_2}{1+3 m_2}=\sqrt{3}$ or $\frac{3-m_2}{1+3 m_2}=-\sqrt{3}$\begin{array}{ll}
\Rightarrow & 3-m_2=\sqrt{3}+3 \sqrt{3} m_2 \\
$\text{ or $} & 3-m_2=-\sqrt{3}-3 \sqrt{3} m_2 \\
\Rightarrow & m_2(1+3 \sqrt{3})=3-\sqrt{3}
\end{array}$ \Rightarrow \quad m_2(1+3 \sqrt{3})=3-\sqrt{3}
or$\quad m_2(1-3 $\sqrt{3})=3+\sqrt{3}\Rightarrow \quad$ m_2=$\frac{3-\sqrt{3}}{1+3 \sqrt{3}} \text{$ or $}  m_2=\frac{3+\sqrt{3}}{1-3 \sqrt{3}}\therefore \quad$ m_2=$\frac{3-\sqrt{3}}{1+3 \sqrt{3}}, \frac{3+\sqrt{3}}{1-3 \sqrt{3}}$

Given, equations of lines are $\sqrt{3}x+y=1 \quad \text{...(i)}$ and $x+\sqrt{3} y=1 \quad \text{...(ii)}$ Let $m_{1} and m_{2}$ be slopes of Eqs. (i) and (ii), then $m_{1}=-\sqrt{3}, m_{2}=-\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}$ Let $\theta$ be angle between them, then $\begin{aligned} \text{$ and $} \tan \theta=\left|\frac{m_{1}-m_{2}}{1+m_{1} m_{2}}\right|=\left|\frac{-\sqrt{3}+\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}}{1+\sqrt{3} \cdot \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}}\right|=\left|\frac{-3+1}{2 \sqrt{3}}\right|=\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}\\
\Rightarrow \theta=\frac{\pi}{6}
\end{aligned}$
